    "alert_id": "ALERT_TDF_PHOSPHATE_LOW",
    "title": "Low phosphate with TDF — possible tubulopathy",
    "message": "TDF can cause renal tubular wasting syndrome, including phosphate wasting. Patient on TDF for 6 years with serum phosphate 0.78 mmol/L and trace proteinuria. Creatinine 112 umol/L is within normal limits but does not exclude early tubular dysfunction. Evidence for drug attribution: TDF is a known cause of tubular wasting, and hypophosphataemia plus proteinuria are characteristic. Evidence against: occupational factors and poor BP control could contribute to proteinuria; phosphate level is only mildly reduced. Guidelines recommend eGFR monitoring at 6–12 monthly intervals on TDF, and more frequent monitoring in high-risk patients including those with co-existent hypertension.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Calculate eGFR; trend phosphate and proteinuria; consider TDF substitution with TAF if tubular dysfunction confirmed. Monitor renal function more frequently given hypertension.",
    "citations": [
      {"page_number": 5, "chunk_id": "sahcs_adult_art_2024__sahcs_adult_art_2024__p5_c3"},
